  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on satellite-derived land surface temperature and estimated evapotranspiration as proxies for ambient air temperature and actual water loss.",
      "what": "Using satellite observations from 2003 to 2024 across 2,911 global cities, this study measured how drought limits the cooling effect of urban vegetation. Greening reduced daytime land surface temperatures by a global summer average of 0.405°C, but this cooling capacity dropped sharply once drought passed specific Palmer Drought Severity Index (PDSI) thresholds, such as -0.827 in summer. Beyond these thresholds, the relationship inverted, with the summer cooling slope shifting from 0.201 to -0.205°C per unit PDSI as moisture constraints limited evapotranspiration.",
      "why": "It establishes that urban vegetation has a non-linear, threshold-limited capacity to mitigate heat, proving that green infrastructure becomes rapidly less effective at cooling cities during the very drought periods when temperature regulation is most needed."
    }
  },
